scissor-py research prototype, 2026-10-09

This distribution contains a Python translation/adaptation of the Scissor
APML1 Net algorithms and new Python preprocessing, validation, diagnostics,
corrected solvers, tests, packaging, and documentation.

Upstream work: Scissor, by Duanchen Sun (author and maintainer).
Repository: https://github.com/sunduanchen/Scissor
Exact source commit: 311560a1f160f665917e7da0fcf289914ee99773
Files used: R/APML1.R, R/net_lm.R, R/net_logit.R, R/net_cox.R,
R/Scissor.R, src/Scissor.cpp, DESCRIPTION, README.md, LICENSE.
Unmodified source snapshots and SHA-256 hashes are included in the source
distribution under reference/upstream and reference/manifest.json.

Scissor DESCRIPTION declares GPL (>= 2). Its README and LICENSE identify
GPL version 3. This prototype is distributed under GPL-3.0-or-later, with
the original GPLv3 license text preserved in LICENSE and the unmodified
upstream license retained in the reference source snapshot. No MIT-license
or upstream-endorsement claim is made.

Modifications made 2026-10-09:
- NumPy/SciPy/pandas implementation; no R runtime or R bridge.
- Sparse, explicitly identified input graph; no automatic graph construction.
- Separate official safe-subset compatibility and explicit corrected semantics.
- Guards for undefined upstream graph indexing and invalid scientific inputs.
- Corrected Gaussian curvature/stopping, exact logistic objective, stable
  Breslow Cox optimizer, aligned cell graph, and actual Cox lambda labels.
- Explicit convergence diagnostics, strict CV candidate failure handling,
  metadata, ID alignment, optional duck-typed AnnData adapter, and test fixtures.
- APML1 L0 post-fit trimming, adaptive penalties/signs, and other penalties
  are not implemented; Scissor's untrimmed Net selection is the scope.

Additional checkpoint modifications made 2026-10-10:
- Reject rectangular graphs before ID alignment and duplicate target IDs.
- Bounded column-block normalization/Pearson retains every input observation.
- Handle actual AnnData backed sparse expression through its public interface.
- Add real AnnData/H5AD input, graph, memory and numeric-boundary tests.
- Preserve original reference evidence and clearly mark full LUAD validation
  and formal Omicverse writeback as unfinished in this checkpoint.
- Explicit complete-LUAD acquisition/preparation scripts and full input hashes;
  independent development R/Rcpp mathematical oracle, without modifying the
  original Scissor source snapshot.
- Corrected Gaussian full-KKT active set and explicitly requested optional
  Numba loop (no fastmath, no parallel JIT, default NumPy path retained).
- Stable extreme-float quantile/Pearson arithmetic and sparse COO duplicate
  edge validation before coalescing. No phenotype or cell is silently dropped.

Quantile-normalization behavior was verified against preprocessCore 1.75.1
by Ben Bolstad, source commit 5f4522fcbce0820b4983f453e3d56341da62da85.
preprocessCore is a development oracle only and is not bundled in the runtime.

